{"subjectId":"python","id":"functional","title":"Lambdas & Functional Tools","group":"Functions","order":12,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Small anonymous functions plus <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>sorted(key=…)</code> and <code>functools</code> — Python's functional toolkit.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lambda\"></i>lambda</h3>\n<p><code>lambda args: expression</code> — a one-expression function with no name, no statements and no annotations. Best as a short <code>key=</code> or callback.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>add = lambda a, b: a + b\nprint(add(2, 3))                                            # 5\nwords = [\"banana\", \"Apple\", \"cherry\"]\nprint(sorted(words, key=str.lower))                         # ['Apple', 'banana', 'cherry']\nprint(sorted(words, key=lambda w: (len(w), w)))             # ['Apple', 'banana', 'cherry']\nprint(max([\"aa\", \"b\", \"cccc\"], key=len))                    # cccc</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-right-left\"></i>map, filter, zip</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(list(map(lambda x: x * 10, nums)))        # [10, 20, 30, 40]\nprint(list(filter(lambda x: x % 2, nums)))      # [1, 3]\nprint(list(map(int, \"123\")))                    # [1, 2, 3]\nprint(list(map(pow, [2, 3], [3, 2])))           # [8, 9]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>map</code>, <code>filter</code> and <code>zip</code> return <strong>lazy iterators</strong>. Wrap them in <code>list()</code> to see the values — and remember they can be consumed only once.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tools\"></i>functools</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import reduce, partial, cache, lru_cache\n\nprint(reduce(lambda acc, x: acc * x, [1, 2, 3, 4], 1))   # 24\n\nbase2 = partial(int, base=2)\nprint(base2(\"1011\"))                                      # 11\n\n@cache\ndef fib(n):\n    return n if n &lt; 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\nprint(fib(80))                                            # 23416728348467685\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=128)\ndef slow_square(x):\n    return x * x\nslow_square(4); slow_square(4)\nprint(slow_square.cache_info().hits)                      # 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i><code>@cache</code> / <code>@lru_cache</code></h3><p>Memoize pure functions — the fastest way to turn a recursive solution into top-down DP.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i><code>partial</code></h3><p>Pre-fill some arguments to create a simpler function.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-minimize\"></i><code>reduce</code></h3><p>Fold a sequence into one value. Often <code>sum</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>math.prod</code> or a loop is clearer.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-settings\"></i><code>operator</code> module</h3><p><code>itemgetter(1)</code>, <code>attrgetter('age')</code> — faster, named alternatives to small lambdas.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from operator import itemgetter\nrows = [(\"b\", 2), (\"a\", 3), (\"c\", 1)]\nprint(sorted(rows, key=itemgetter(1)))    # [('c', 1), ('b', 2), ('a', 3)]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Prefer a comprehension over <code>map</code>/<code>filter</code> with a lambda — <code>[x * 2 for x in xs]</code> reads better than <code>list(map(lambda x: x * 2, xs))</code>.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Assigning lambdas to names (<code>f = lambda x: …</code>) in real code — use <code>def</code> so the function has a name in tracebacks.</p></div></div></section>"}